{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Read-only investigation complete. The requested split was performed and it refutes the ticket's own premise in two ways.\n\n## Correction: the population is 4, not ~52\n\nThe ticket is framed around \"~52 failures\" of a single kind. That conflated distinct causes. Against unmodified `origin/main` in the sandbox, `make test` produced **51 total failures, of which only 4 are SemLock**. The other 47 are separate, unrelated sandbox problems — `pypandoc`/pandoc execution, `ps` denial, and git identity assumptions among them. They were not root-caused, since this ticket's requested split is SemLock-specific, but they must not keep being counted as part of this defect.\n\n## The split: runner-level is zero\n\n| Population | Count | Modules |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Runner-level pytest-xdist | **0** | none |\n| Test-level multiprocessing | **4** | `tests/unit/test_network_guard_local_ipc.py`, `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_atomic_completion_close.py`, `tests/unit/test_marker_fcntl_guard.py`, `tests/scripts/graph/test_doctor_locks.py` |\n\nxdist is **not** affected. `pytest -n 4 --dist worksteal` brought its worker pool up successfully and reached 99%; no SemLock traceback originated from xdist startup or coordination. The 4 test-level failures persisted under `pytest -p no:xdist`, and those 4 exact nodeids fail in 41.56s standalone — proving they are not runner-level parallelism.\n\nThis matters for the remedy: **changing the worker count cannot fix anything**, because the runner-level population it would address is empty.\n\n## Confirmed root cause\n\nThe sandbox denies Darwin named POSIX semaphore creation (`sem_open`) with `EPERM`, and CPython `multiprocessing` has no usable fallback on this host because `sem_init` returns `ENOSYS`.\n\nA standalone C probe outside pytest:\n\n```\nsem_open(/mp38223probe, O_CREAT|O_EXCL, 0600, 1) failed errno=1 Operation not permitted\nsem_init failed errno=78 Function not implemented\n```\n\nDirect `_multiprocessing.SemLock(...)` calls failed identically for every legal name shape tested, while ordinary file creation in the worktree succeeded — so this is a denial of the semaphore syscall itself, not of a backing-file path.\n\nRepresentative traceback:\n\n```text\nFAILED tests/unit/test_network_guard_local_ipc.py::test_network_guard_allows_forkserver_multiprocessing\n    ctx = mp.get_context(\"forkserver\")\n>   ready = ctx.Event()\nmultiprocessing/synchronize.py:57: in __init__\n    sl = self._semlock = _multiprocessing.SemLock(\nE   PermissionError: [Errno 1] Operation not permitted\n```\n\nNo start method avoids it. `spawn`, `fork`, and `forkserver` all fail constructing `Lock`, `RLock`, `Semaphore`, `BoundedSemaphore`, `Event`, `Queue`, and `Pool`. `ThreadPoolExecutor` works but is not a process-isolation substitute. `multiprocessing.dummy.Pool` also fails, because in this Python it still constructs a multiprocessing `SimpleQueue`.\n\n## `PYTEST_WORKERS=1` is not the answer\n\nIt does not even bypass xdist: `make test` hardcodes `-n $(PYTEST_WORKERS) --dist worksteal`, so `PYTEST_WORKERS=1` runs `-n 1`, still under xdist. The run passed lint, typecheck and import-convention, then logged failures by 3% of the suite. It was stopped and reaped at 42.61 minutes while still at 19%.\n\nFull single-worker wall-clock is therefore **unverified** — the run did not complete. It was terminated because it had already failed and completing several more hours could not make it a green gate, not because a runtime measurement was obtained.\n\n## Existing handling\n\n`Makefile`: `PYTEST_WORKERS ?= 4`, `verify: lint typecheck import-convention test`, and `test` hardcodes the xdist invocation above. `tests/conftest.py` already sandboxes `REBAR_ROOT` and gate temp dirs, and its network guard already permits AF_UNIX for forkserver IPC — but there is **no** POSIX semaphore capability probe and no SemLock or sandbox handling anywhere.\n\n## Fix direction\n\nNot the worker count. Add a direct **capability probe** — attempt `SemLock` / `sem_open` and observe the result, satisfying the no-environment-heuristics constraint — and handle only the 4 affected tests explicitly. Prefer rewriting a test to avoid `multiprocessing` wherever process fidelity is genuinely preserved; where it is not, mark it as requiring POSIX named-semaphore capability and emit a **loud, counted** signal on the sandbox path, never a silent skip. CI's `-n 4` full coverage stays unchanged.\n\n## The gate is still not restored by this\n\nStating this plainly because it bears on the ticket's escalation clause: fixing all 4 SemLock failures does **not** make `make verify` green in the sandbox. The other 47 failures remain, from at least three further capability denials. This ticket, as scoped, removes one of several blockers. Restoring `make verify` as a local gate under the sandbox requires the remaining 47 to be triaged as well, and that is not in scope here. A follow-up ticket for that triage has a clean boundary and would let this one close on its own terms.\n\nNo code changes were made."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789685376716311864,"uuid":"96292ca0-b621-41d5-bc16-4cf5b38d7bbe"}